616 0 obj
<</Title<FEFF004D0069006C0069007400610072007900200046006F00720074002022C5002000310038003500390020002D00200031003900300037>/Dest[6 0 R/Fit]/Parent 498 0 R/Prev 599 0 R/Next 600 0 R>>
endobj

617 0 obj
<</Title(After 1819 the British claimed the hill and renamed it Government Hill. In 1823 Sir Stamford Raffles built his residence here, and the summit became the home of successive colonial governors. The area also developed as a centre of scientific interest, administration and communication.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 606 0 R>>
endobj

618 0 obj
<</Title(Raffles Garden commemorates Raffles\220 interest in natural history and the first botanical experiments on the hill.)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 589 0 R/Prev 607 0 R/Next 608 0 R>>
endobj

619 0 obj
<</Title(Field Tip:)/Dest[8 0 R/Fit]/Parent 608 0 R>>
endobj

620 0 obj
<</Title(Gothic Gate)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 504 0 R/Prev 609 0 R/Next 621 0 R>>
endobj

621 0 obj
<</Title(A Cemetery on the Hill)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 504 0 R/Prev 620 0 R/First 627 0 R/Last 627 0 R/Count -1/Next 610 0 R>>
endobj

622 0 obj
<</Title(The Cemetery Wall defined the burial ground, while the Gothic Gate and Cupolas formed its distinctive entrance and structures.)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 610 0 R/Next 623 0 R>>
endobj

623 0 obj
<</Title(The Napier Monument survives as one of the best-known memorials from the former cemetery, preserving the memory of an early colonial family.)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 610 0 R/Prev 622 0 R/First 628 0 R/Last 628 0 R/Count -1>>
endobj

624 0 obj
<</Title(In the late 1850s the British converted Government Hill into a fortified stronghold to defend Singapore. The new citadel was named Fort Canning after Charles John Canning, and its walls, gates and artillery were designed to command the harbour approaches. The fort remained in military use until it was decommissioned in 1907.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 612 0 R>>
endobj

625 0 obj
<</Title(The Old Gunpowder Magazine stored ammunition, while the Sally Port provided a controlled secondary exit.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 594 0 R/Prev 613 0 R/Next 614 0 R>>
endobj

626 0 obj
<</Title(Field Tip:)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 614 0 R>>
endobj

627 0 obj
<</Title(Fort Canning once held Singapore\220s earliest Christian cemetery, used in the first decades of the colonial settlement. Important residents, soldiers and children were buried here between the 1820s and mid-19th century. Although most graves were later cleared, several architectural remnants survive.)/Dest[9 0 R/Fit]/Parent 621 0 R>>
endobj

